msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Algorithmiq's Tensor-network Error Mitigation (TEM) method is a hybrid "
"quantum-classical algorithm that removes the effect of hardware noise "
"entirely in classical post-processing. The circuit runs on the QPU as "
"written, with an informationally complete (IC) measurement at the end. A "
"tensor network that inverts the learned noise of the device is then applied "
"to the measurement outcomes, giving error-mitigated expectation values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial reproduces, at 31 qubits, the kind of many-body dynamics "
"experiment run on up to 91 qubits in [L. E. Fischer et al., Nat. Phys. "
"(2026)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-025-03144-9). It shows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"How much signal hardware noise destroys in a utility-scale dynamics circuit,"
" and how much TEM recovers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"How TEM compares with probabilistic error cancellation (PEC) and zero-noise "
"extrapolation (ZNE) on the same circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The IC measurement also makes it possible to estimate observables that were "
"not requested when the circuit ran, with a rerun of only the classical post-"
"processing. For an example that estimates the full light cone of a chain, "
"see [Run 4: measure the light cone from the same "
"data](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/functions/algorithmiq-tem/performance-"
"tuning#run-4-measure-the-light-cone-from-the-same-data).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To tune TEM's accuracy against its QPU and classical cost, see the companion"
" example [Tune the TEM function for accuracy and "
"cost](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/functions/algorithmiq-tem/performance-"
"tuning).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Usage estimate: approximately 20 QPU seconds for TEM on `ibm_phoenix` (the "
"QPU time depends heavily on the chosen device). The optional PEC and ZNE "
"comparison takes about 35 QPU minutes more, almost all of it for PEC.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Background: the kicked Ising chain {#background-the-kicked-ising-chain}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The kicked Ising model alternates an Ising evolution on a chain of $N$ "
"qubits,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$ \\hat{H}_{\\text{I}} = J \\sum_{n=0}^{N-2} \\hat{Z}_n \\hat{Z}_{n+1} + h "
"\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} \\hat{Z}_n , $$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "with a transverse kick,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$ \\hat{H}_{K} = b \\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} \\hat{X}_n . $$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One period of the dynamics is the Floquet unitary $\\hat{U}_{\\text{KI}} = "
"e^{-i \\hat{H}_K} e^{-i \\hat{H}_I}$. On a quantum computer each period is a"
" brickwork of two-qubit $ZZ$ rotations followed by single-qubit rotations, "
"so the circuit contains only two distinct layers of two-qubit gates, "
"repeated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The quantity of interest is the infinite-temperature autocorrelation "
"function of the first spin. The [reference "
"paper](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-025-03144-9) shows that it can"
" be measured by preparing the first qubit in $|+\\rangle$ and every other "
"pair of qubits in the Bell state $(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$, "
"evolving for $t$ periods, and measuring a single-qubit Pauli operator "
"$\\hat{X}_n$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At the dual unitary point $|J| = |b| = \\pi/4$ the model is exactly "
"solvable:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The correlation travels along the chain at maximal speed, so after $t$ "
"periods only qubit $n = t$ carries it and $\\langle \\hat{X}_{n \\neq t} "
"\\rangle = 0$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On that qubit, $\\langle \\hat{X}_{n=t} \\rangle = [\\cos(2h)]^t$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"These exact values make the model a sharp benchmark for error mitigation: "
"the signal that reaches qubit $t$ has passed through every layer of the "
"circuit, so it is exposed to all the noise of the device, yet its noiseless "
"value is known. Away from the dual unitary point the same circuits become "
"hard to simulate classically, which is where the paper uses TEM to go beyond"
" exact methods.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"TEM works in three steps: it learns the noise of each distinct layer of two-"
"qubit gates and the readout error, runs the circuit with an IC measurement, "
"and finally applies the noise-inverting tensor network to the measurement "
"outcomes on IBM Cloud&reg;.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Setup {#setup}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As a prerequisite, ensure that the necessary dependencies are installed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Build the circuit and the observables {#step-1-build-the-circuit-"
"and-the-observables}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses a circuit that implements the kicked Ising model described in the previous section. The circuit is prepared as follows. First, there is a state preparation phase, in which the first qubit is in the state $|+\\rangle$, while the others are in Bell pairs $(|00\\rangle + |11\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$. This is followed by the brickwork structure that implements the unitary evolution $\\hat{U}_{\\text{KI}}$. The number of physical time steps corresponds to $t/2$ circuit layers.\n"
"The following code builds the circuit used in this tutorial.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, build the observable, $\\hat{X}_{n=t}$. It is constructed as a simple "
"Pauli string with the order matching the one used by Qiskit:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The experiment uses 31 qubits and 15 periods at $h = 0$, so the exact value "
"of $\\langle \\hat{X}_{n=15} \\rangle$ is 1. This is a utility-scale circuit"
" with 465 two-qubit gates: 15 that prepare the Bell pairs and 30 in each "
"period. The signal that reaches qubit 15 passes through hundreds of them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2: Run TEM on the QPU {#step-2-run-tem-on-the-qpu}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, get access to the TEM function and select a backend from the available"
" devices. This code assumes you've already [saved your "
"account](/guides/functions-get-started#install-qiskit-functions-catalog-"
"client) to your local environment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Load the TEM function from the [IBM Quantum&reg; Functions "
"Catalog](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/functions):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Transpile the circuit for the chosen device once, and map the observable "
"onto the qubits it was placed on. TEM accepts this instruction set "
"architecture (ISA) circuit as it is, and the PEC and ZNE comparison in Step "
"3 uses the same circuit, so all methods run the same gates on the same "
"qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following options are based on the physics of the circuit:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**`max_layers_to_learn: 4`**: an upper bound on the number of distinct "
"layers of two-qubit gates whose noise is learned. The brickwork has only two"
" distinct layers, so only those two are learned.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**`default_shots: 100_000`**: the number of shots of the IC measurement, "
"which sets the statistical precision of the estimate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**`tem_max_bond_dimension: 128`** and **`tem_compression_cutoff: 1e-14`**: a"
" moderate bond dimension that keeps the classical post-processing fast at 31"
" qubits, with a small cutoff so that the bond dimension is not truncated "
"further.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**`private: False`**: keeps the job results downloadable later. Set it to "
"`True` for confidential circuits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The [performance tuning "
"example](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/functions/algorithmiq-tem/performance-"
"tuning) explains each of these options in detail.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Calculate the function size that the job needs with the size calculator "
"provided by IBM&reg;, and pass it to `tem.run` as `function_size`. The "
"calculator maps the circuit onto the backend and returns `S`, `M`, or `L` "
"without submitting anything; see [Function "
"sizes](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/functions/algorithmiq-tem/get-"
"started#function-sizes).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, run the experiment, get the result, and visualize it. This step "
"takes approximately 20 QPU seconds on `ibm_phoenix`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Save the job ID. Use it to retrieve the results later with "
"`catalog.get_job_by_id(job_id)`, or to rerun the post-processing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 3: Compare with PEC and ZNE {#step-3-compare-with-pec-and-zne}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The result metadata also contains the unmitigated estimate, computed from the same measurement outcomes without the noise-inverting network. A comparison of the two with the exact value shows how much of the signal the noise destroys, and how much TEM recovers:\n"
"To put the TEM result in context, run the same circuit with two other error mitigation methods: probabilistic error cancellation (PEC) and zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) with probabilistic error amplification (PEA).\n"
"<Admonition type=\"caution\">\n"
"  The following code runs a large PEC experiment, which requires around 40 minutes of QPU time.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> **Note:** Each mitigation method can produce expectation values that are "
"either higher or lower than the exact result. The standard deviations "
"reflect statistical uncertainty only; they do not account for inaccuracies "
"in the characterized noise model, because that model has no associated error"
" bound.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The unmitigated value decays with the number of noisy gates in the light "
"cone of qubit 15, so at this depth most of the signal is lost. TEM removes "
"this decay in post-processing, recovering most of the signal without running"
" any additional circuits. The remaining gap to the exact value comes from "
"the speed-oriented settings used in this tutorial: a bond dimension of 128 "
"and a modest noise-learning budget. The [performance tuning "
"example](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/functions/algorithmiq-tem/performance-"
"tuning) shows how to trade QPU and classical time for accuracy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Next steps {#next-steps}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Change $h$ in the circuit to move away from the exact value $[\\cos(2h)]^t ="
" 1$, or away from the dual unitary point, where classical simulation becomes"
" hard. See [L. E. Fischer et al., Nat. Phys. "
"(2026)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41567-025-03144-9) for results up "
"to 91 qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Learn how each TEM option affects accuracy, QPU time, and classical time in "
"[Tune the TEM function for accuracy and "
"cost](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/functions/algorithmiq-tem/performance-"
"tuning).\n"
msgstr ""

